<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nicomedes IV</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 94–74 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Nicomedes IV Philopator</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nicomedes IV Philopator was the last king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bithynia">Bithynia</a> in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. Twice driven out by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-vi-of-pontus">Mithridates VI of Pontus</a>, he was restored each time by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. On his death in 74 BCE he left his kingdom to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> in his will, and the bequest provoked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-mithridatic-war">Third Mithridatic War</a>. Bithynia became a Roman province.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nicomedes-iv.jpg" alt="Nicomedes IV" width="320" height="304" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ArchaiOptix · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bithynia_-_king_Nikomedes_IV_-_94-74_BC_-_silver_tetradrachm_-_head_of_Nikomedes_IV_-_Zeus_-_M%C3%BCnchen_SMS_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
